nswer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516246} :answer-field/id #uuid "99dc7578-e9a5-5def-a6d4-42f491265aea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62333d8a-a547-57b1-ac3c-5c56714cd529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62333d8a-a547-57b1-ac3c-5c56714cd529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516326} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81e3147-4f8f-5dc1-aa20-2dd74fcc8034" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a8971db-b741-5f69-8444-adec12e8e30e" :question/math-academy-id "q-252634" :question/problem "Express the equation ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ in exponential form. Write the exponential term on the left-hand side of the equation.\n\n{{field-1}}$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{125},$ $b=5,$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{5}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e579e2a-cf55-5dc2-9631-36dd85e6533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516209} {:answer/id #uuid "69bd26b6-6e3c-5c38-a3bc-9ab6d641cd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516342} {:answer/id #uuid "987d4650-fafb-51a4-b9a2-850870587748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516427} {:answer/id #uuid "b57bb91e-bc6a-52e3-9e91-2b526a2371b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{20}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987d4650-fafb-51a4-b9a2-850870587748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516427} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca20e363-d96f-5c7c-870a-e0d6761fc82a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e09043a-2656-5006-b96f-e232ecdf95b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344045" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\pi \\,\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{5}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{5}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{5}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n\\sqrt{5}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d8cfe9-4b93-5f75-80a5-535b163dfe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516214} {:answer/id #uuid "34d6d3c7-1689-5248-895a-b81d7cf970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516223} {:answer/id #uuid "6466cbf3-e0e7-5e28-a7cc-3d9c12cae870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516331} {:answer/id #uuid "66956326-a3a4-5914-80b0-99ab80e8cc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516334} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cebdaf-ad45-5124-8538-17b343686fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cebdaf-ad45-5124-8538-17b343686fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a92c6e-2892-5988-ae79-425c23feccca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7012a871-ceb2-5bf5-9d51-cddf37c78344" :question/math-academy-id "q-133054" :question/problem "The average volume of soda in cans is $12$ ounces. Additionally, in $3%$ of cans, the soda volume is less than $11.7$ ounces. Assuming the volume of soda is normally distributed, find the percentage of cans containing less than $12.2$ ounces of soda, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the volume of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12.2).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<11.7)=0.03.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11.7) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.7-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)≈-1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & =-1.8808 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =6.26933 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{6.26933} \\\\  & ≈0.1595.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12.2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12.2) & =P(X\\le 12.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12.2-12}{0.1595}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25) \\\\  & ≈89.4%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $89.4%$  of soda cans contain less than $12.2$ ounces of soda."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516113} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516267} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516405} {:answer/id #uuid "8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516411} :answer-field/id #uuid "51585168-5999-52ac-a302-915344f4951e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-344269" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-6,6]$, where $f$ is $12$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $12$-periodic on $[-6,6]$, we have $L=6$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dac4121-cbe6-553e-bd16-6fab29fec507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516143} {:answer/id #uuid "380efcee-b3e5-51ba-b34a-0999cc8d3601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{3-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516228} {:answer/id #uuid "4f793398-fc2c-5c69-a5eb-7e3e8a2143ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516275} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e05fce-5995-5a9b-8200-cbf720d49a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516554} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbcdb37-7a76-5a7a-b5f5-5c2b7570f9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{3-{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dac4121-cbe6-553e-bd16-6fab29fec507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516143} :answer-field/id #uuid "6744b580-35a3-5368-ab4c-1be0682aa649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc18352d-fce3-5063-b58a-47ac19e5cf8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95512" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{{x}^{12}}{9})}\\cdot 2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4dc213e-ad65-5a21-812e-d696846cae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4dc213e-ad65-5a21-812e-d696846cae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516487} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ade9f6-f749-5ec9-893d-33df04f83e39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c561c471-e505-55e6-9a2d-28f93bd6cab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-252619" :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${b}^{-3}=a,$  then $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=64,$ $b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-3}=64.$ Consequently, $a=64.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d48108-d2d6-587b-bfe3-2ffd1073736d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516156} {:answer/id #uuid "2606f266-1daa-5d7d-8ca5-771dec8b5b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516185} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf03bd5-254e-5534-bc31-f8df36341221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516311} {:answer/id #uuid "a54621c5-4d43-5cff-83c2-5c5c66d64ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516456} {:answer/id #uuid "f6449119-7803-5c83-b8f8-f21cb84631f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d48108-d2d6-587b-bfe3-2ffd1073736d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516156} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b3a12d-2d4a-5204-9a4e-bc2880e6dedc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca24054b-57db-5111-abb2-9a1c0e62e7da" :question/math-academy-id "q-95428" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=6\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2796cfc5-c5b4-5036-9f76-eece180c2cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516194} {:answer/id #uuid "33ec99ac-f5a9-54c6-98e8-d72f51562c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516221} {:answer/id #uuid "4374fd25-2d74-5431-80a5-28f72721f792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516248} {:answer/id #uuid "901f86ad-20b1-5cdb-a778-0edeead6c1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516415} {:answer/id #uuid "b43f7a5c-b51d-5889-ab8d-1480bbb2e7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ec99ac-f5a9-54c6-98e8-d72f51562c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516221} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed5d147f-c842-54bd-aa6e-2a8c2bb0981a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1b18b73-e150-5d64-9819-aec4cb39257d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71161" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (-3{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-3{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02150670-428b-56e2-9985-6194ff4b8ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516116} {:answer/id #uuid "0c56636a-f906-5735-8c37-7b2138e8f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516136} {:answer/id #uuid "4562e56f-79a7-5953-82dc-cdefccb01ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} {:answer/id #uuid "bf315d70-83f5-5ea5-abd9-3ae3cfc47fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516517} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2bf34-d828-5bcd-970b-c54ad54643d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4562e56f-79a7-5953-82dc-cdefccb01ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} :answer-field/id #uuid "701ad097-1360-59f4-8891-d19c7b1ca762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4a129bc-20d0-5e0c-8e4d-26304c226b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-118558" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of beavers in a river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}),\\,y(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible beaver population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}) \\\\  & =0.2y\\left(\\frac{800-y}{100}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2y}{100}(800-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the number of beavers cannot exceed $800.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b3afc9-f9cc-5c8a-b6ab-4390c55f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517120} {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} {:answer/id #uuid "98fc6441-004a-50b8-be13-2c8d2fa211bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517130} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f20f9-ffc8-5085-b0e1-8fa22e17eee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517131} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f14205-061b-58e6-8ec2-15a66df7a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,\\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} :answer-field/id #uuid "95633b9d-4569-53c0-963e-77e08465b5f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8cb0f82-4ced-5ad3-8499-c17a38448a7a" :question/problem "The point $(-8,7)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-8,7)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-5:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,7)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (7))=(-8,\\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,\\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right))=(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,- \\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}-5)=(-8,- \\frac{27}{4}).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,7)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n4f(-8)=7\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-8)=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-8)-5\\\\&=-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)-5\\\\&=- \\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ada36c-1091-5f42-8c63-eccc8b641f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$255$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517327} {:answer/id #uuid "b937214f-6d30-5777-98d4-63d7c6dc1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-105$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517358} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2e00ae-4e6a-5d71-aef7-bdb151691a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-75$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517359} {:answer/id #uuid "cc796484-ad70-51d7-bb01-5b8342bff754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$180$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517363} {:answer/id #uuid "d16bf7d6-ca47-5047-90d1-e323cc45a658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$105$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d16bf7d6-ca47-5047-90d1-e323cc45a658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$105$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517367} :answer-field/id #uuid "60454d3c-fec5-59e1-9526-d25e77d4da41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f4724c3-2921-55f4-a281-340d20927d04" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=140t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=90+15\\sin(2\\pi t/14)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $18\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(1110-480)/60=10.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(10.5)-C(10.5) & =140(10.5){e}^{-0.2(10.5)}-(90+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{14}(10.5))) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-90-15\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-75 \\\\  & \\approx 105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $105$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b221c18-6d59-5ed7-9a36-5ef2cdd6198b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$228$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517325} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea5bf78-0a77-58d1-9e2a-bacef9819834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-228$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517326} {:answer/id #uuid "5baff62d-cf1c-57ec-b9d7-d7a353611316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$288$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517336} {:answer/id #uuid "9b301213-2943-5d08-a28e-082e30a5e73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$348$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517351} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d8817e-e114-506e-a9b2-a1055123a32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-60$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b221c18-6d59-5ed7-9a36-5ef2cdd6198b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$228$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517325} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf15e5a0-9fea-5686-9f0a-1969e5135c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e5ec36a-ecef-5d7c-8e17-1882b5fa8983" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=190t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=50+10\\sin(2\\pi t/10)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $10\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(630-480)/60=2.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(2.5)-C(2.5) & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.2(2.5)}-(50+10\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}(2.5))) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-50-10\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-60 \\\\  & \\approx 228.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $228$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6c992b-a9bf-50ec-a63b-953cb1055324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-121$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517328} {:answer/id #uuid "48c1409d-fc69-54ea-b9a0-2713986d307a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-35$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517333} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb55a74-3f47-50c3-a675-afc6dec9b4f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$121$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517337} {:answer/id #uuid "b03e3130-ebeb-56a0-a735-21aa477c71ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$156$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517354} {:answer/id #uuid "de1c0611-7856-5339-b7b2-d83aedf32026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$191$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17